","known_bad_label":"Sorts the caller-owned input array in place instead of sorting an independent sequence."},{"id":"cap-v2-sequence_algorithms-typescript-repair-02","problem":"Repair the source below and return one complete TypeScript source file.

Export the function `splitOnSeparators(values, separator)`. It partitions a sequence of finite numbers whenever `separator` occurs, omitting the separators themselves. Each separator terminates one segment, so leading, trailing, and consecutive separators produce empty segments. With no separators, the result contains one segment holding all values.

Every result segment must be a distinct array: mutating one segment must not affect any other segment. The input must remain unchanged. Do not use filesystem, network, clock, randomness, packages, or frameworks.

The current implementation aliases its output segments:

```typescript
export function splitOnSeparators(
  values: readonly number[],
  separator: number,
): number[][] {
  const segments: number[][] = [];
  const current: number[] = [];

  for (const value of values) {
    if (value === separator) {
      segments.push(current);
      current.length = 0;
    } else {
      current.push(value);
    }
  }

  segments.push(current);
  return segments;
}
```","reference_code":"export function splitOnSeparators(
  values: readonly number[],
  separator: number,
): number[][] {
  const segments: number[][] = [];
  let current: number[] = [];

  for (const value of values) {
    if (value === separator) {
      segments.push(current);
      current = [];
    } else {
      current.push(value);
    }
  }

  segments.push(current);
  return segments;
}
