","known_bad_label":"Accepts any present `approved` value and coerces it to boolean instead of rejecting non-boolean malformed input."},{"id":"cap-v2-stateful_workflows-typescript-repair-01","problem":"Repair the source below and return one complete TypeScript source file.

The file must preserve this public API:

```typescript
export type TaskState = "QUEUED" | "RUNNING" | "COMPLETED";

export class TaskScheduler {
  enqueue(id: string, priority: number): void;
  startNext(): string | undefined;
  complete(id: string): void;
  getState(id: string): TaskState | undefined;
}
```

Callers provide non-empty unique IDs and finite priorities. `enqueue` creates a `QUEUED` task. `startNext` selects the queued task with the greatest priority, changes it to `RUNNING`, and returns its ID. Among equal-priority tasks it must select in original enqueue order, regardless of their IDs or of earlier task completions. It returns `undefined` when nothing is queued. `complete` changes a `RUNNING` task to `COMPLETED`; otherwise it throws `Task is not running: <id>` without changing any task. `getState` reports the current state.

Do not use filesystem, network, clock, randomness, packages, or frameworks.

The current implementation violates the equal-priority ordering rule:

```typescript
export type TaskState = "QUEUED" | "RUNNING" | "COMPLETED";

interface TaskRecord {
  id: string;
  priority: number;
  order: number;
  state: TaskState;
}

export class TaskScheduler {
  private readonly tasks = new Map<string, TaskRecord>();
  private nextOrder = 0;

  enqueue(id: string, priority: number): void {
    this.tasks.set(id, {
      id,
      priority,
      order: this.nextOrder++,
      state: "QUEUED",
    });
  }

  startNext(): string | undefined {
    const queued = [...this.tasks.values()].filter(
      (task) => task.state === "QUEUED",
    );
    queued.sort(
      (left, right) =>
        right.priority - left.priority || left.id.localeCompare(right.id),
    );

    const selected = queued[0];
    if (selected === undefined) {
      return undefined;
    }
    selected.state = "RUNNING";
    return selected.id;
  }

  complete(id: string): void {
    const task = this.tasks.get(id);
    if (task === undefined || task.state !== "RUNNING") {
      throw new Error(`Task is not running: ${id}`);
    }
    task.state = "COMPLETED";
  }

  getState(id: string): TaskState | undefined {
    return this.tasks.get(id)?.state;
  }
}
