Tasks: [{"id":"cap-v2-stateful_workflows-typescript-repair-02","problem":"Repair the following TypeScript implementation of an isolated resource workflow.

The public API is exactly:

- `JobState`: `"queued" | "running" | "completed" | "rolled_back"`
- `JobSpec`: readonly `id`, `priority`, and `units` fields, all with the types shown in the source
- `JobView` and `WorkflowSnapshot` as shown
- `ResourceWorkflow` with `constructor(capacity)`, `submit(spec)`, `complete(id)`, `rollback(id)`, and `snapshot()`

Inputs obey these preconditions: capacity is positive; every submitted ID is unique; units are positive and no greater than capacity; priorities are finite.

A submitted job starts immediately if no job is running. Otherwise, it remains queued. Whenever the running job completes or rolls back, the queued job with the greatest priority starts. Jobs tied on priority must start in their original submission order. At most one job may run at a time.

Starting a job reserves its units. Both completion and rollback release those units before the next job starts. `complete` performs `running -> completed`; `rollback` performs `running -> rolled_back`. Either method must throw `Error("Job is not running")` for any other transition. Snapshots list jobs in submission order and report the currently running ID or `null`.

The source has exactly one semantic defect. Return a corrected complete source file.

```typescript
export type JobState = "queued" | "running" | "completed" | "rolled_back";

export interface JobSpec {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly priority: number;
  readonly units: number;
}

export interface JobView {
  readonly id: string;
  readonly state: JobState;
}

export interface WorkflowSnapshot {
  readonly availableUnits: number;
  readonly running: string | null;
  readonly jobs: ReadonlyArray<JobView>;
}

interface JobRecord {
  readonly spec: JobSpec;
  readonly sequence: number;
  state: JobState;
}

export class ResourceWorkflow {
  private readonly capacity: number;
  private availableUnits: number;
  private readonly jobs: JobRecord[] = [];
  private nextSequence = 0;

  constructor(capacity: number) {
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.availableUnits = capacity;
  }

  submit(spec: JobSpec): WorkflowSnapshot {
    this.jobs.push({
      spec: { id: spec.id, priority: spec.priority, units: spec.units },
      sequence: this.nextSequence++,
      state: "queued"
    });
    this.startNext();
    return this.snapshot();
  }

  complete(id: string): WorkflowSnapshot {
    const job = this.runningJob(id);
    job.state = "completed";
    this.availableUnits += job.spec.units;
    this.startNext();
    return this.snapshot();
  }

  rollback(id: string): WorkflowSnapshot {
    const job = this.runningJob(id);
    job.state = "rolled_back";
    this.availableUnits += job.spec.units;
    this.startNext();
    return this.snapshot();
  }

  snapshot(): WorkflowSnapshot {
    const running = this.jobs.find((job) => job.state === "running");
    return {
      availableUnits: this.availableUnits,
      running: running?.spec.id ?? null,
      jobs: this.jobs.map((job) => ({ id: job.spec.id, state: job.state }))
    };
  }

  private runningJob(id: string): JobRecord {
    const job = this.jobs.find(
      (candidate) => candidate.spec.id === id && candidate.state === "running"
    );
    if (job === undefined) {
      throw new Error("Job is not running");
    }
    return job;
  }

  private startNext(): void {
    if (this.jobs.some((job) => job.state === "running")) {
      return;
    }

    const next = this.jobs
      .filter((job) => job.state === "queued")
      .sort(
        (left, right) =>
          right.spec.priority - left.spec.priority ||
          right.sequence - left.sequence
      )[0];

    if (next !== undefined) {
      next.state = "running";
      this.availableUnits = this.capacity - next.spec.units;
    }
  }
}
